1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,755
Pepper on water —
why does it run away?

2
00:00:04,805 --> 00:00:06,825
Pour a little water
into a wide plate

3
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:08,975
Sprinkle pepper
all over it

4
00:00:09,025 --> 00:00:11,125
Dab a little dish soap
on your fingertip

5
00:00:11,175 --> 00:00:14,695
Keep soap away from your eyes
and wash your hands after!

6
00:00:14,745 --> 00:00:16,665
First, touch it with a clean finger

7
00:00:16,715 --> 00:00:18,835
…nothing happens

8
00:00:18,885 --> 00:00:23,305
Now the soapy finger!
Count to three and touch

9
00:00:23,355 --> 00:00:27,405
The pepper rushes
to the edges!

10
00:00:27,455 --> 00:00:29,475
The water at the surface
holds on and pulls together

11
00:00:29,525 --> 00:00:31,645
So the surface acts like a stretched skin
(surface tension)

12
00:00:31,695 --> 00:00:33,745
Where the soap touches,
the pull gets weaker

13
00:00:33,795 --> 00:00:37,945
The stronger skin around it pulls outward
and carries the pepper along

14
00:00:37,995 --> 00:00:42,345
That stretched skin is also
how water striders stand on water!

15
00:00:42,395 --> 00:00:44,795
Just a drop of soap, and wash your hands after!

16
00:00:44,845 --> 00:00:47,495
Arrow · paper · pepper —
how many did you try?
